<h2>Part 1: Introduction, Visual Identity &amp; Authentication (0:00 &ndash; 0:50)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Screen is on https://pingcraft-gamma.vercel.app at the Sign In page.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Hello everyone, my name is Prakrit Sangwan, and today I&rsquo;m presenting PingCraft &mdash; an end-to-end synthetic API health monitoring and incident response platform built on the MERN stack.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
The problem PingCraft solves is critical: APIs frequently suffer silent degradation, latency spikes, or intermittent 500 errors, and engineering teams usually don&rsquo;t find out until users complain. PingCraft continuously probes critical endpoints, measures high-precision latency, triggers automated incident lifecycles when SLA thresholds breach, and publishes public status bulletins for transparency.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Before logging in, notice our visual identity: we deliberately stepped away from generic, AI-generated dark navy templates. Instead, PingCraft uses an <strong>Industrial Editorial</strong> design language &mdash; inspired by precision measurement instruments, warm paper typography, stone dividing rules, and technical notebooks.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
On this authentication screen, any new user can register with email and password, which uses bcrypt password hashing with salt rounds on our backend. Since I already have my account set up, let me log right in.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Enter email and password, then click &lsquo;Sign In&rsquo;.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Once logged in, notice my username displayed right here at the top corner alongside our session controls.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
<strong>Why this works technically:</strong> upon successful authentication, our Express backend generates a signed JSON Web Token containing the user&rsquo;s ID and name. Our React AuthContext decodes this payload, keeps it in memory and local storage, and attaches it as a Bearer authorization header to every subsequent API request while displaying our active session status right here.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>Part 2: Cockpit Dashboard &amp; Instant Probe with SSRF Defense (0:50 &ndash; 1:50)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Land on the main Monitors Cockpit dashboard.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;This brings us to the Monitors Cockpit.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Notice the layout:
</p>
<ul class="spoken">
  <li>At the top, we have system-wide KPIs: Total Monitors, Operational, Degraded, Outages, and Paused services, rendered with tabular monospace numbers and calibrated earth tones &mdash; muted forest green for healthy endpoints, ochre for degraded, and brick red for active outages.</li>
  <li>Instead of bulky cards, the monitors are organized into an editorial register table separated by clean stone dividing rules.</li>
</ul>

<p class="spoken">
Before setting up recurring background probes, PingCraft includes an on-demand Instant Probe diagnostic instrument. Let me open it.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click &lsquo;Instant Probe&rsquo; button. Modal pops up.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;This modal lets engineers immediately probe an endpoint. But more importantly, this is where our custom SSRF Protection Engine comes into play. Because synthetic monitors accept arbitrary URLs, attackers often try to query internal loopback addresses or cloud metadata.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Let&rsquo;s test our defense. If I try to target loopback at <span class="badge">http://localhost:5000</span> or an AWS metadata IP like <span class="badge">169.254.169.254</span>...&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Type http://localhost:5000/api/health and click &lsquo;Execute Probe&rsquo;.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;...the request is immediately blocked by our backend DNS and subnet validator before any socket connection is opened.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Now let&rsquo;s test a legitimate public endpoint, like <span class="badge">https://api.github.com</span>.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Type https://api.github.com and click &lsquo;Execute Probe&rsquo;.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Within milliseconds, we get an industrial diagnostic readout: HTTP 200 OK, the exact latency measured with Node&rsquo;s monotonic <span class="badge">performance.now()</span> clock, payload size in bytes, and sanitized headers.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>Part 3: Monitor Registry &amp; Deep Telemetry Analytics (1:50 &ndash; 2:50)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Close Instant Probe modal. Click on a monitor row to open Monitor Detail.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Creating a synthetic monitor is simple: clicking &lsquo;Add Monitor&rsquo; lets us configure the HTTP method, custom request headers, check interval &mdash; such as every 15 or 30 seconds &mdash; and consecutive failure SLA thresholds.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Let&rsquo;s click into one of our active monitors to view its deep telemetry page.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Monitor Detail view is now visible.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Here in the detail view:
</p>
<ul class="spoken">
  <li>The page heading is set in an editorial serif typeface, pairing aesthetic restraint with dense engineering telemetry.</li>
  <li>Up top, we have rolling 24-hour and 7-day uptime percentages computed by our dedicated backend metrics service. Notice that if an endpoint lacks sufficient historical data, it honestly displays &lsquo;No data&rsquo; rather than faking 100%.</li>
  <li>Below that is our Latency Trend chart on a soft ivory canvas with a vermilion trace line, plotting latency history alongside our calculated 95th percentile (p95) latency using the nearest-rank mathematical method.</li>
  <li>Further down is the ping telemetry log, displaying precision UTC timestamps, HTTP status codes, response times, and failure classifications for every probe.</li>
  <li>We can also pause the monitor anytime with one click, which instantly excludes it from the scheduler workload.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>

<h2>Part 4: Incident War Room &amp; State Machine (2:50 &ndash; 3:45)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click &lsquo;Incident War Room&rsquo; tab in the top contrast navigation bar.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Next, let&rsquo;s inspect the Incident War Room.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
When an endpoint fails its consecutive failure threshold &mdash; for example, 2 consecutive failed checks &mdash; the monitor automatically transitions from DEGRADED to OUTAGE, and PingCraft&rsquo;s deterministic state machine opens an incident record.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Our MongoDB database enforces partial unique compound indexing, ensuring that a monitor can have at most one active incident at any time, preventing alert fatigue and duplicate spam.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Responders can manage the incident lifecycle through a structured workflow: moving from <span class="badge">TRIGGERED</span> to <span class="badge">INVESTIGATING</span> with root-cause notes, then to <span class="badge">MITIGATING</span>, and finally to <span class="badge">RESOLVED</span>.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click an active incident, type an investigation note, click &lsquo;Acknowledge &amp; Investigate&rsquo;, show timeline.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Every status change appends an event to this immutable audit timeline, recording the actor, timestamp, and update note.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Even better: when the service recovers and passes consecutive probes meeting its recovery threshold, PingCraft&rsquo;s background scheduler automatically resolves the incident, logs the exact downtime duration in seconds, and restores the monitor to OPERATIONAL without human intervention.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>Part 5: Branded Public Status Bulletin (3:45 &ndash; 4:15)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click &lsquo;Public Status Page&rsquo; in navigation, then click &lsquo;View Live Page&rsquo; or open /#/status/api-status.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;For transparent communication with users and stakeholders, PingCraft provides Public Status Bulletins.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
From the admin settings, we can choose our custom URL slug and select exactly which components are visible externally.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Opening the live page: notice how this public bulletin is styled like an official technical notice printed on warm paper. It is completely unauthenticated, reveals zero API keys or server secrets, and gives users real-time component health, uptime percentages, and historical incident transparency.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>Part 6: Tech Stack &amp; Automated Test Suite (4:15 &ndash; 4:45)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Switch window to VS Code or Terminal displaying &lsquo;npm test&rsquo; output.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Under the hood, the entire architecture is built on the modern MERN stack:
</p>
<ul class="spoken">
  <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> React 18, Vite, and Tailwind CSS with custom editorial design tokens, deployed live on Vercel.</li>
  <li><strong>Backend:</strong> Node.js and Express, hosted on Render.</li>
  <li><strong>Database:</strong> MongoDB Atlas with Mongoose, utilizing compound indexes for fast telemetry queries and partial indexes for incident integrity.</li>
  <li><strong>Engine:</strong> A non-blocking background scheduler running concurrent synthetic probes with monotonic high-resolution latency timers and an SSRF security shield.</li>
</ul>

<p class="spoken">
To ensure production-grade reliability, the codebase is backed by a comprehensive test suite. As you can see right here in my terminal: all 35 unit and integration tests in Jest and Supertest &mdash; covering the SSRF defense engine, p95 percentile calculations, the incident state machine, and end-to-end API workflows &mdash; are passing with zero errors.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Both the live Vercel application and the GitHub repository are linked in the description. Thank you for watching!&rdquo;
</p>

